WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.150
- Welcome, welcome to another Fed Day,

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:06.660
FOMC day edition of "Market Takes."

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.400
I know you could have been
anywhere in the world,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.560
but you're here with me
and I appreciate that.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.400
Thank you so much.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.060
I am Dion Rabouin, reporter
at the "Wall Street Journal."

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.290
This is "Market Takes."

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.040
We are diving into today's
FOMC press conference,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.010
the statement, the decision, all that.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.080
We are gonna break down
what all of it means

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.520
and explain why it matters.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.580
Today's meeting I think was
a little bit more subdued,

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.420
a little bit, you know,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:31.980
you didn't have a lot of the big fireworks

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.570
that we've come to expect from Fed Day.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.150
But, nonetheless, there is
some very important context

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.790
and we are gonna get into
right here on "Market Takes."

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.070
We're gonna talk about, of
course, the Fed rate decision,

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.020
and how that aligned with expectations.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.000
We're gonna talk through
what's going on in the markets,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.220
how markets reacted across stocks, bonds,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.950
currencies, commodities,
all that good stuff.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:50.790
And, of course, we're gonna talk about

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.260
the environment coming into the meeting,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.630
and how today's meeting
and press conference

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.640
and all that may have
shifted things going forward.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.920
And then of, course, we will
talk about what comes next

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.960
'cause, you know, even though
it's backward looking data,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.270
we are always looking forward.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.130
Anyway. So looking, let's get to it.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.810
The Fed decision today,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.600
the FOMC raised rates by 25 basis points.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.150
This was widely expected,
widely priced in,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.290
not surprising anybody.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.333
The Fed raising rates from 4.75 to 5%

00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:20.670
to 5% to 5.25%, effectively 5.1%.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.440
Wanna put a chart up here on your screen

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.520
so you can just look for yourself

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.670
and see, kind of, the environment
that we have right now.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.570
All the way on your right

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.180
you see how high the rates have gotten.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.680
We are back above 5%.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.180
That is the highest the rates have been

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.610
since September of 2007.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.210
So, almost 16 full years since
we've had rates this high.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.970
And you see what happened
after rates were that high,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.380
they came back down pretty quick.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.770
We had a recession, as you
see with there, shaded in.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.370
That is not necessarily indicative
of what will happen now,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.160
past results are not
indicative of future results

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.480
but we shall see.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.654
It's definitely gonna be interesting.

00:01:56.654 --> 00:01:58.740
We are entering the interesting times now,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.780
the times where things
get a little bit harder,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.030
a little more difficult for the Fed,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.230
for Fed chair Jerome Powell.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.540
So, that charge is kind of illustrating

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.270
where we are in terms of the Fed rate

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.040
and how that compares with recent history,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.680
at least over the past 20 years.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.530
A big thing that I think was
interesting from this report,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.410
and my biggest takeaway
in terms of the statement,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.210
was the change that, instead of saying,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.730
there's some additional firming ready

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.110
that that could be done,

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.423
the statement now says,

00:02:26.423 --> 00:02:29.430
"The Fed is determining the extent

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.567
to which some additional policy
firming may be appropriate."

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.470
RSM Chief Economist,
Joe Bruce Willis, said,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.530
they were "Opening the
door to a strategic pause

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:41.760
at the June meeting."

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.260
Basically that was market's takeaway.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.630
The Fed said that they
were going to, kind of,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.210
reassess, reevaluate, they
were gonna hike today.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.170
But at the press conference,

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.827
even Fed chair Jerome Powell said,

00:02:50.827 --> 00:02:53.310
"The changes in the
statement were intentional."

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.110
They made those changes to say,

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.820
look, it's time we kind of step back.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.590
We've been raising rates every meeting.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.990
Ten straight meetings
they've increased rates,

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.780
including raising 75 basis points

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.340
three times the normal pace

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.200
at four meetings earlier this year.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.910
So, some big changes were made

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.800
and now we are entering a new phase

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.210
of this rate hiking cycle.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.380
And that's what I think you're seeing

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.510
the market respond to right now.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.523
Coming into this meeting today,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.880
things have been pretty
straightforward and easy, right?

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.610
Now, it's not necessarily
easy to raise rates

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.380
because, of course, markets want low rates

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.000
but with inflation being high,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.850
it got up to as high as 9% in June.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.430
Of course, the Fed has to raise
rates - it's pretty simple.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.430
Inflation goes up, the Fed has
to respond by raising rates.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.460
They put it off in 2021
saying, transitory, transitory,

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.410
we think it's gonna go away on its own.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:41.430
When that didn't happen

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.790
it became clear they had to
raise rates, they did that.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.440
Now we're entering the phase

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.930
where it's not gonna be so easy anymore.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.600
You had high inflation, low unemployment,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.160
well in that case you raise rates.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:53.993
It's very simple.

00:03:53.993 --> 00:03:56.340
And the unemployment rate
didn't pick up very much

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.050
as they've been raising rates.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.970
What you were starting to see in the data,

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.340
and we'll get more into
this in just a second,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.690
is the unemployment data

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.340
starting to move in
the opposite direction.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.440
You're seeing job losses start to pick up.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.300
You're seeing inflation
start to come down,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.030
and now the Fed really
has to make a hard choice

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.970
or start making some hard
choices in the next few months.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.790
So the Fed was really
setting the table for that

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:18.990
coming into today.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.900
Now we had seen an environment
where stocks had been rising.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.500
You saw the S&amp;P 500 up
around 7% year-to-date,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.730
it had been up almost...

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.550
It had reached a pretty
high point recently.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.350
Nasdaq up about 15%

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.830
hitting its highest in
September 12th, 2022 on Friday.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.470
Really almost all of this
growth happened in January

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.330
but we have seen stocks picking up.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.700
Bond yields on the other side going down.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.440
That for laymans out there means

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.120
an easing of financial conditions.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.890
When they say financial conditions easing,

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:47.723
that's what they mean.

00:04:47.723 --> 00:04:49.890
Making it easier for
companies to issue stock

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.330
because stock prices are high

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.310
or to borrow money because
bond yields are low.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.780
That makes it cheaper to borrow.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.880
That's what we're seeing
right now out there

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.070
in the economy for big companies.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.840
And we are starting to
move into an environment

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.610
where things could be a little tighter,

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.220
a little more uncertain as we go forward.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.790
So, want to throw up another
thing on your screen right here

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.860
and this is the Fed's balance sheet.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.430
Now, what you've seen over
the past few years is,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.110
you see the balance sheet just pick up

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.110
and hit these extreme levels
where we're passing 5 trillion,

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.150
6 trillion, 7 trillion, 8 trillion,

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:22.680
getting almost to $9 trillion.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.720
And then we had what was known as QT

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.390
sort of start to begin late last year.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.100
And then when this
banking crisis happened,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.350
you see that pickup
there right at the end,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.700
and that was the Fed injecting

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.490
another $300 billion into the system,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:36.420
into the banking system,

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.430
to try to stem these outflows

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.700
and worries in the banking
sector about collapsing

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:45.180
and the, you know, some
contagion in terms of banks.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.920
A lot of loans made to big banks,

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.540
a lot of buying up of bad assets.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.220
But now we are seeing that reverse again

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.350
and you're seeing the line
starting to point down.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.480
We're seeing the beginning of QT again,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.488
aka quantitative tightening.

00:05:56.488 --> 00:05:58.170
That just illustrates

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.530
the way the Fed's balance
sheet has moved long term,

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.210
and we are entering a phase now

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.150
where the Fed doesn't have
the wind at its back anymore.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.130
You know, instead of putting that,

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.920
injecting money into the financial system

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:11.850
as they had been doing,

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.530
getting that balance
sheet all the way up there

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.030
to near $9 trillion,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.870
we are, in theory at least,
going to be moving down

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.180
where they're taking money
out of the financial system

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.310
while holding rates high.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.290
And that is gonna weigh on the economy.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:27.870
So, like I said, the easy part is over.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.880
Now we are moving into the harder part.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.010
I wanna put one more thing
up here on your screen

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.960
and it's just some more economic data.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.870
This is the comparison of job openings

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.270
versus unemployed people in the US.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.220
And Fed chair Jerome Powell

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.237
has mentioned this a
number of times, he said,

00:06:43.237 --> 00:06:44.940
"Just today, during his press conference,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.370
there's still 1.6 unemployed people

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.550
or 1.6 job openings for
every unemployed person."

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:54.060
So even if we found jobs
for every single person

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.890
counted as unemployed in the country,

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.590
there would still be
millions of job openings

00:06:58.590 --> 00:06:59.970
left over to be unfilled.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.360
Keep in mind, whether
someone is unemployed or not

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.400
does not determine whether
they're unemployed.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.480
That doesn't make any sense.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:07.980
Here's what I mean by that.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.300
Someone could be out of a job

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.040
but if they're not
actively looking for a job,

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.560
if they are not telling the
Bureau of Labor Statistics

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.140
I am out here looking for a
job and I just can't find one.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.190
If they're not checking in

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:19.380
with some sort of
government agency saying,

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:20.550
I'm looking for a job,

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.790
they're not unemployed, they
are out of the labor force.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.830
And what we have seen is
a significant increase

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.870
in the number of people
out of the labor force

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.610
just not being counted.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.230
A lot of those people, working age people,

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:35.280
between 25 and 55,
so-called prime age workers,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.450
they are not unemployed.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.770
They're just out of the labor force

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:38.970
if they're not looking for a job.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:41.010
So, the number of unemployed people

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.310
has been declining pretty significantly

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.550
over the past 20 years and we
saw that speed up after COVID.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.070
So again, you're seeing the
number of job openings decline

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.260
but there's still a lot
of job openings out there.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:53.580
A lot of work looking,

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.350
or a lot of people looking for workers,

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:56.910
and a lot of work to be done.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:58.920
This chart illustrating that trend.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.350
But you're seeing a decline
in the number of job openings

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.390
and you're seeing that
number of unemployed people

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.680
stay pretty steady

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.050
because while some people are
coming back into labor force,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.430
other people are getting laid off,

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:09.750
and that's why we see, kind of,

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.430
that line not really moving.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:14.400
So, now let's take a look
at the market right now.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.200
And let's take a look at what we're seeing

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:17.520
from stock prices.

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:18.780
Now look, we've got this overview.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:19.950
I'm checking out commodities.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.180
I want to take you back to the overview

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:22.290
and let's look at the stock market.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.160
This is the DOW.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.860
You're seeing the DOW
down about 0.8% right now.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.800
Compare that to the NASDAQ
down about half as much.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.810
But you see the same trend, right?

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.874
Whether you're looking
at the DOW, the S&amp;P 500,

00:08:32.874 --> 00:08:35.220
or the NASDAQ, all three.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:36.053
This is the DOW.

00:08:36.053 --> 00:08:37.860
And you see right around two o'clock

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.030
when the Fed releases its statement,

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.050
the market picks up.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.270
And then you see the statement come on,

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:43.860
the press conference starts about 2:30,

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.860
and then you see things
just really start to unravel

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.630
and the sales just pick up

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.340
and people are selling outta those Stocks.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.350
I think this is just a
little bit of a buy the room

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:53.370
or sell the news.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.590
Folks expected this
statement to be dovish.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:58.620
Folks expected the Fed to
raise rates by 25 basis points.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.380
And when I say dovish, what
I mean is the Fed saying,

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.510
okay, we think things are gonna be easier.

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:06.090
We are leaning towards keeping rates lower

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:07.800
or pushing rates lower in the future.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.303
The market has priced in

00:09:09.303 --> 00:09:12.120
that the Fed is going to start
cutting rates later on today.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:13.380
And what we're seeing right now

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:16.620
I think illustrates the
expectations from markets,

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:17.700
and from investors,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.620
that the Fed is going to start moving away

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.320
from raising rates, moving
towards cutting rates.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.140
We see that with Fed fund futures prices

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.840
as we look out across the market.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:28.980
And we're seeing it with commodity.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.480
I think the big moves that we've seen

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.740
over the past couple days,

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.010
oil prices are down about 10%
over the last couple days.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:35.843
You're seeing that

00:09:35.843 --> 00:09:38.100
and that was why we started
off with commodities prices,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.380
oil down big, natural gas down big.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:44.010
Those commodities, which are
very sensitive to the economy

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:47.820
are starting to move lower in
a drastic and significant way.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.590
We aren't seeing it in stock prices yet.

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.750
We've seen it in bonds in the bond market

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.550
over the past couple weeks,

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.560
but we aren't seeing in Stocks just yet.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.570
So, definitely something
to keep your eye on.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.730
I think what we're seeing
right now in commodities

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.860
is important and
definitely worth watching.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.230
So, let's talk about the
press conference really quick,

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.233
because that was something
I think that, you know,

00:10:06.233 --> 00:10:08.820
there weren't a lot of
fireworks, as I said earlier.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.520
There wasn't any big blowout bombshells

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.750
from Fed chair Jerome Powell.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.190
He really just kind of laid it out.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.080
We think now is the time to pause.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:17.700
We're gonna be data dependent,

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.070
meaning that depending on
what happens with the data

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:22.890
we will act well, we can
raise rates, we can cut rates,

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.360
we can keep rates where they are.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.820
But I think right now
the bias for the Fed,

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.140
based on what we heard from the chairman

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:30.180
and what we read in that statement,

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:31.920
that came out around two o'clock,

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.560
is that the Fed's bias is to hold pat,

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.360
not raise rates, not cut them

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:39.000
but to just hold them here
at this very high level.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.260
The market does not agree.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.900
The market thinks that we are
going to get, potentially,

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:44.400
another increase in July

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.800
and then a cut in September,
a cut in November,

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.180
and a cut in December,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.650
as the Fed starts to ease up

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.390
on those financial conditions again.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.300
Remember, financial conditions were eased

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:56.280
by the Fed pumping money
out into the economy

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.240
into the banks,

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.240
even though they didn't engage
in any quantitative easing

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.530
or cut rates.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.900
When the Fed pushes money
into the banking system

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.760
that eases financial conditions,

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.650
makes it easier for banks to lend money

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:08.910
to those small businesses,

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.960
to do more borrowing and
to get the economy flowing.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:14.400
Now they're reversing all that,

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.040
as we saw with the Fed balance sheet chart

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.260
I put up here on your
screen a little earlier.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:20.760
All of the things the Fed has done

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.250
to ease financial conditions
are starting to come off

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:25.890
and that likely is going
to affect market soon

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:28.713
and certainly is going to
affect the real economy.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.180
And, so this today's press
conference, today's statement,

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.100
I think really just set in motion,

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.800
all right, we are
resetting where things are

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.950
and now we are moving into
this new phase of the economy,

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:43.020
of the job market, and
all that good stuff.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:46.320
But again, it will be all about the data.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.630
The Fed says they're data
dependent, believe them

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.910
because if the data comes in surprisingly,

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:54.030
if the data comes in above
expectations, below expectations,

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:55.680
you can expect the Fed to react

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.170
and you can expect
markets to react to that.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.300
That takes us to our
next point really well,

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.040
which is what comes next?

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:05.520
Well, tomorrow we get the
Challenger Job Cuts report.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:06.960
That comes out early in the morning.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:07.793
You're gonna wanna check that out.

00:12:07.793 --> 00:12:10.650
That's from private agency
Challenger Grand Christmas.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.150
They track the number of layoffs,

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:15.180
aka job cut announcements, aka job cuts.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.030
That has picked up pretty
significantly as we've started

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.620
and moved into this year.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.830
We have seen 400% increase in March

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:25.590
from last year where job cuts were.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:28.710
And we'll see if layoffs
continue to pick up steam.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.542
That's not great news for the economy.

00:12:30.542 --> 00:12:33.180
Then, on Friday, we get the Jobs report,

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.950
we will be right here for "Market Takes,"

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:36.477
breaking down what we've seen

00:12:36.477 --> 00:12:39.060
and what to make of that job report.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:39.930
Fed chair Jerome Powell

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.790
pointed out during his
press conference today,

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:44.670
there's been an average
of 345,000 jobs added

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.160
over the first three months,
the first quarter of this year.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.470
That is a very high
pace, a very fast pace,

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:51.300
and the Fed wants to slow that down.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.360
They want fewer people getting
jobs, they want fewer raises,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.260
they want less wage growth
among the folks in the economy.

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:00.480
I know that sounds strange,
but that's what they want

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.430
because they need to slow inflation down.

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:04.770
So, Jobs Friday is gonna be big.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.900
We hope you'll be with us
right here on "Market Takes".

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.360
Then next week we've got
our look ahead on Monday.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.580
Again, "Market Takes" baby, don't miss it.

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.310
And on Wednesday the other big kahuna

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:17.310
coming out will be CPI,
the Consumer Price Index,

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:19.830
that report is gonna tell us
what's going on with inflation

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:21.270
and all eyes will be on that

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.600
as the Fed moves back towards
its data dependent stance.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.130
No more... We can't just say,

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:27.480
okay the Fed's gonna raise this month

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.820
because, you know, that's
what they've been doing.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:30.653
That's all over.

00:13:30.653 --> 00:13:33.780
We are moving into a new regime
where a report, like CPI,

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:37.500
the Jobs report, PPI,
any number of reports,

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.110
the Economic Cost Index could move the Fed

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.270
to raise cut, hold pat,

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:43.950
and you're gonna wanna watch that data

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.120
because that's gonna be big.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.280
That's what the Fed is
depending on moving forward.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:48.870
Anyway, that's gonna wrap it up for us

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:50.250
here on "Market Takes".

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:51.480
I'm your host, Dion Rabouin.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.400
I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:54.420
but you're here with me.

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:56.010
I hope you'll do it again on Friday

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:57.360
when we come back right here

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.260
for another edition of "Market Takes".

